---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/manifesto.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  preloadSerif
  title="AI Manifesto"
  description="Why I build AI: a personal essay on mental health, running 600 AI agents at once, and the engineering principles I hold to — human problems first, evidence over hype, tools for researchers, and open artifacts."
>
  <header class="manifesto-hero" aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">AI Manifesto · Why I build AI</p>
    <h1 id="manifesto-title">It didn’t start with AI.<br /><em>It started with a concussion.</em></h1>
    <p class="manifesto-lead">
      I build AI to help people, not replace them. Understanding what that means to me took several changes of direction and a failed attempt at an AI tutor.
    </p>
    <img class="page-photo" src="/assets/raul-cardenas-montoya-404-512.jpg" srcset="/assets/raul-cardenas-montoya-404-256.jpg 256w, /assets/raul-cardenas-montoya-404-512.jpg 512w" sizes="8rem" width="512" height="512" alt="Raul smiling in a car" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <nav class="manifesto-jump" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#why-i-build-ai">01 · Why I build AI</a>
      <a href="#turning-into-a-doomer">02 · Turning into a doomer</a>
      <a href="#not-a-doomer">03 · Now, I am not a doomer</a>
      <a href="#my-hope">04 · My hope</a>
      <a href="#principles">05 · Commitments</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <article class="manifesto-article" data-manifesto-article>
    <section class="manifesto-chapter" id="why-i-build-ai" aria-labelledby="chapter-why">
      <header class="manifesto-chapter-header">
        <p class="manifesto-chapter-num">01 · Origin</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-why">Why I build AI</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="manifesto-prose">
        <p>
          In 7th grade, a hit in football changed my brain, and my mental health never recovered through middle school or high school. So, in college, I wanted to be a psychiatrist. Not for status or money. I wanted to solve the problems that are hardest to see, because I had lived inside one. The catch was biology. I couldn’t remember the terms; I failed the class, and that failure sent me back to studying business administration at Austin Community College.
        </p>
        <p>
          Later, I admitted the thing I’d been avoiding: I wanted to be an engineer and didn’t believe I could be one. So, I took the plunge and started Computer Science at Concordia University Texas. That semester, almost every day I heard that AI was replacing programmers. The fear got to me. I jumped ship to Electrical Engineering, applied to 30-plus schools, and landed at Texas State.
        </p>
        <p>
          Texas State was a mess. Admissions made me reapply more than once, and I wasn’t enrolled until two or three weeks into the semester. I’d just built an expensive workstation, so I had an idea: use it to build a local AI tutor and catch up. I was sure it would be a game-changer. Originally, I was writing the code, then I even had AI coding agents — programs that write code on their own — build it for me, so I figured I’d have time left over for my hobbies. That time never came.
        </p>
        <p>
          I spent more time building the tutor than studying, and I had to drop my courses. For the love of God, I did my best to make sure I wouldn’t owe anything. I submitted my withdrawal on March 31st — at TXST, the last day to withdraw without receiving a failing grade. But April 1st was the day that you can withdraw without owing anything. I hit one deadline and missed the other by a day. Then an email came in saying I owed money, and I was angry enough to switch schools a third time. After a year of running from AI, I chose AI Engineering at WGU.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="manifesto-chapter" id="turning-into-a-doomer" aria-labelledby="chapter-doomer">
      <header class="manifesto-chapter-header">
        <p class="manifesto-chapter-num">02 · Isolation &amp; the 600-agent test</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-doomer">Turning into a doomer</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="manifesto-prose">
        <p>
          It wasn’t just TXST. Losing and gaining weight over the years, and seeing how differently people treated me each time, had already pulled me away from people. The fallout at TXST and my anger at the system solidified that withdrawal. My heart hardened as I isolated myself — I had turned into a doomer, someone who thinks this all ends badly — and I stopped caring whether AI replaced people’s jobs. I went all in on ChatGPT, Claude, and Grok subscriptions; basically, I tried every AI I could get my hands on. I started to believe AI could fill the gaps people used to fill. The less I thought I needed human interaction, the more isolated I became. Funny enough, AI itself pushed back on that belief. Using it for coding, and even for talking through my anger and resentment, made me realize I still need people in my life.
        </p>
        <p>
          On Sept. 15, 2026, I put that belief to the test. I had around 600 AI agents writing and checking code for me at once, trying to squeeze every last drop out of my monthly plan before it reset. They did an enormous amount of work. The bottleneck was me because someone still had to check it all. That night did more to shake my belief that AI could replace humans than anything I had read. Yes, they could do a lot of work, but that comes at a cost.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="manifesto-pullquote">
          <p>
            How do you grade homework that the student has already graded? Well, you must grade it yourself. The more they could do, the more it mattered that a human who could judge the work was checking it. I wasn’t less necessary that night. I was the point.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="manifesto-chapter" id="not-a-doomer" aria-labelledby="chapter-not-doomer">
      <header class="manifesto-chapter-header">
        <p class="manifesto-chapter-num">03 · Clarity</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-not-doomer">Now, I am not a doomer</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="manifesto-prose">
        <p>
          After all of that, I don’t believe AI is going to take everyone’s job, and I don’t believe it is going to end human civilization. But a lot of people online do, and the fear is eating them alive. Jacob Coxon, a researcher who had worked at Anthropic and OpenAI, two of the biggest AI labs, quit and posted that the people building AI “earnestly believe that it could kill us all by the end of the decade.” People online panicked and started giving up on life over it. Seeing that honestly made me sad for people who were genuinely torn by the news. One social media user told me, “I guess I should tell my daughter that there is no future,” which stabbed my heart. My new passion for AI was to use it to empower other human beings. So seeing AI turn into a political fight, instead of a tool for helping people, hurt me.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="manifesto-pullquote">
          <p>
            If you’re reading this scared that there’s no future for you in any of this — I believed that about myself for years. I won’t tell you everything will be fine. I’ll tell you the truth I have: there’s still a place for you. I found mine on the fourth try.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="manifesto-chapter" id="my-hope" aria-labelledby="chapter-hope">
      <header class="manifesto-chapter-header">
        <p class="manifesto-chapter-num">04 · Purpose</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-hope">My hope</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="manifesto-prose">
        <p>
          My biggest hope is that AI helps researchers make discoveries in mental health that we can’t make today – the kind of pattern that only shows up across thousands of patients and that no single doctor could ever spot on their own.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most likely it won’t be me making the discoveries. I am an engineer, not a psychiatrist or biologist. But I know what it is like to be a patient. That is why I started with the concussion, and why I told you about the class I failed. If I ever see someone use my work to make a discovery that leads to better treatment for mental health patients, I will cry. That is what I am building for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="manifesto-chapter" id="principles" aria-labelledby="chapter-principles">
      <header class="manifesto-chapter-header">
        <p class="manifesto-chapter-num">05 · Commitments</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-principles">What I’ll hold to as an AI Engineer</h2>
      </header>
      <ol class="manifesto-principles" role="list">
        <li class="manifesto-principle">
          <span class="manifesto-principle-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <div class="manifesto-principle-body">
            <p><strong>Human problems first.</strong> Humans created AI, so we must use it to help people live better.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="manifesto-principle">
          <span class="manifesto-principle-index" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <div class="manifesto-principle-body">
            <p><strong>Evidence over fear, and over hype.</strong> If I say something works, I show the proof: what I tried, what failed, and the numbers. If I can’t show it, I don’t say it.</p>
            <a class="manifesto-proof-link" href="/work/#agoge-forger">Proof: agoge-forger case study (measured results, ties, and regressions) <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="manifesto-principle">
          <span class="manifesto-principle-index" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <div class="manifesto-principle-body">
            <p><strong>Tools for researchers, not replacements for them.</strong> The goal is for a human to make a discovery they couldn’t have made alone, whether they work at a company or a school.</p>
            <a class="manifesto-proof-link" href="/work/#synthetic-factory">Proof: synthetic-factory research-tooling case study <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="manifesto-principle">
          <span class="manifesto-principle-index" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
          <div class="manifesto-principle-body">
            <p><strong>Open where I can.</strong> Share the data and tools so others can check my work, build on it, and prove me wrong.</p>
            <a class="manifesto-proof-link" href="/artifacts/">Proof: published Rust crates, Hugging Face datasets, and merged PRs <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <footer class="manifesto-coda">
        <p>
          I failed the biology class on the path toward becoming a psychiatrist. Now I want to build tools that help researchers make discoveries—and help patients like me.
        </p>
      </footer>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
